// id type -> C type. Any list type is the generic IdList*; a function type is
// a pointer to a function value's object, IdFn*, whatever its signature --
// every function value, plain or a closure, is one (runtime/closure/text.id),
// and the signature is spelled only where a call through the value casts `fn`
// to the type it is called as (c_clo_ptr). That is what lets a closure and a
// plain function be the same type, and a library take either. Otherwise the
// scalar's spelling is a lookup in this target's own column of the type
// registry, not a chain of `if`.
//
// docs/BACKENDS.md asks for exactly this: mid/types/registry/ holds the facts that
// are true of a type in any target (kind, width, signedness) and each target
// owns one column of spellings beside it. The column lives here rather than
// in the registry because "long long" is not a fact about `word`, it is a
// fact about C.

c_type(string t) {
  string ct = "IdList*";
  if(ty_is_func(t) == 1) {
    ct = "IdFn*";
  } else if(ty_is_list(t) == 0 && ty_kind_is(t, "struct") == 0) {
    ct = c_scalar(t);
  }
} return string ct;
given lc_env2 ("int[]"):("IdList*")
given sd_reg ("P"):("IdList*")
given lc_env2 ("func(int) return int"):("IdFn*")
given lc_env2 ("func() return void"):("IdFn*")

// A type with no registry row has no spelling either. It answers `int` for
// the same reason the old chain ended there -- nothing upstream emits a type
// the checks have not already accepted -- but it is now one place rather than
// four, and it cannot be reached by forgetting a case.
c_scalar(string t) {
  int r = find_str((import tyname), t);
  string ct = "int";
  if(r >= 0) {
    ct = (import tyc)[r];
  }
} return string ct;
given lc_env2 ("string"):("char*")
given lc_env2 ("float"):("double")
given lc_env2 ("int"):("int")
